it's no secret that people are crazy  about eggs  we can't seem to stop eating them  in july 2018 the u.s produced more than  7.9 billion eggs for consumption  that's more eggs than there are people  on the planet  but shopping for eggs can be a little  confusing  what do all these different labels mean  are brown eggs different than white eggs  which eggs are the best deal for your  money which are the most humane and  finally are eggs good for you  this  is the conscious consumer's guide to  eggs  let's start with sizes the five primary  sizes of eggs you're likely to see in  the grocery store are small medium large  extra large and jumbo these sizes are  set under guidelines by the usda and  they're determined by weight per dozen  you can expect a small carton to weigh  about 18 ounces medium 21 ounces large  24 ounces extra large 27 ounces and  jumbo 30 ounces these weight classes  take into account the weight of the  entire carton not necessarily the  individual eggs so don't be surprised if  you find a small egg in your jumbo  carton  like snowflakes all eggs are unique and  special  you may have noticed that some egg  cartons in the store are issued grades  not all curtains will have these grades  aren't mandatory and any companies who  get their eggs graded actually pay the  usda for this service the eggs can be  categorized into three grades grade a a  a and b the grades are determined by the  quality of the whites and yolks as well  as what the outer shell looks like us  grade a a is the highest of the three  grades and it's given to eggs that have  thick whites nice round yolks and  unblemished shells grade a a is best if  you're frying or poaching an egg really  it's good for any dish where appearance  is very important  u.s grade a eggs which you'll typically  find at the grocery store are pretty  similar to grade a a eggs the only real  difference is that they have reasonably  firm whites as opposed to very firm  whites the whites of eggs categorized as  us grade b tend to be thinner and the  yolks may not be perfectly round and  while the shells won't be broken they  might not look perfect either grade b  eggs are typically used to make liquid  or dried egg products  as of 2017 about 84 of hens that lay  eggs in the u.s live in battery cages  densely packed cages that don't give  hens any chance to really roam outside  about 16 percent of egg laying hens in  the u.s are cage-free hens which means  the hens live in indoor houses and are  free to roam both vertically and  horizontally the usda's definition of  cage-free also requires that the  chickens have access to fresh food and  water  the usda's definition of free range  means that hens must not only be able to  roam both vertically and horizontally  but they must also be given free access  to the outdoors while they're producing  eggs this outdoor area is allowed to be  fenced in and must keep the chickens  safe from predators  both cage-free and free-range eggs are  grouped together in statistics  additionally there are a few labels used  for eggs that are actually issued  through non-profits three big ones you  might have seen are american humane  certified certified humane and animal  welfare approved now these labels don't  follow any set meaning from the  government and companies pay to have  these labels added to their products  most chicken eggs are either brown or  white  some can even be bluish green it's all  based on the hen's breed for example  like horn chickens tend to lay white  colored eggs whereas plymouth rock  chickens tend to lay brown colored eggs  it's just a matter of what pigments the  hen produces so is there a nutritional  difference between colors not really  nutritionally speaking eggs are very  similar regardless of color one factor  that can impact the nutritional makeup  of an egg the hand's environment a 2014  study published in the journal nutrition  found that eggs produced from free-range  hens have three to four times the amount  of vitamin d compared to cage raised  hands that are kept indoors and although  there isn't much of a nutritional  difference between brown and white eggs  brown eggs tend to be a little more  expensive what gives  the hens that lay brown eggs tend to be  larger and according to usda research  food technologist deanna jones they need  more nutrients in their bodies to  produce the pigments so more feed more  color more expensive  but what about yolks  the color of an egg yolk is primarily  determined by the hen's diet certain  plants or additives used in chicken feed  contain yellow orange pigments called  xanthophylls when ingested by a hen  these pigments give the yolk a rich  yellow color some producers even add  marigold petals carrots or other  additives to their feed to give their  hands eggs a rich yellow-orange color  hens that are fed a wheat and barley  diet on the other hand tend to produce a  paler color yolk white cornmeal diets  can even result in a yolk that's  virtually devoid of color  so now that we know all of that one  polarizing question remains are eggs  good for you  it's been the subject of heated debate  since the late 1970s around that time  doctors began realizing that cholesterol  you know that fatty substance that's  important for digestion and cell  function can put us at risk for heart  disease when consumed in excess a lot  decided then that high cholesterol foods  like butter red meat and yes even eggs  should be avoided  [Music]  and while consuming cholesterol can  increase levels of it in the blood a  growing number of studies according to  npr have shown that most of the  cholesterol in our bodies is heavily  influenced by things like genetics  gender age and overeating things like  trans fats and sugars  dr walter willett a harvard medical  school professor has studied thousands  of patients and hasn't found evidence  that a moderate level of egg consumption  increases one's risk for heart disease  except for those already at risk due to  genetics  at the end of the day there's no black  and white answer  as willett told npr it is hard to say  that eggs are good or bad in terms of  health they seem to be in the middle  somewhere  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

